    "title": "LIVE performance to kickoff the start of 'Traditions Weeks' at McDaniel College",
    "text": "Music rooted in Maryland’s folk traditions will take center stage this spring as Common Ground on the Hill founder and Executive Director Walt Michael promotes two long-running events: the Deer Creek Fiddlers’ Convention and Traditions Weeks.Michael, a multi-instrumentalist best known for playing the hammered dulcimer, played guitar and was accompanied by fiddler Sarah Larsen, an instructor at Common Ground on the Hill. for a LIVE performance on FOX45. Michael has performed at the 13th Winter Olympics, the White House, and the Kennedy and Lincoln Centers. He founded Common Ground on the Hill in 1994. The organization is an independent 501(c)3 nonprofit based on the campus of McDaniel College in Westminster and is one of eight regional folklife centers of the Maryland State Arts Council.One of the events Michael is promoting is the 55th Annual Deer Creek Fiddlers’ Convention, scheduled for Saturday, May 30, at the Carroll County Farm Museum. The convention includes band and individual competitions with cash, instrument and performance prizes. The event has been awarded the Maryland State Heritage Award by the Maryland State Arts Council. More information is available at https://www.commongroundonthehill.org/festival/deer-creek-fiddlers-convention.Michael is also promoting the 32nd Annual Traditions Weeks at McDaniel College in Westminster, featuring two individual sessions of week-long workshops. Organizers say the program includes singing and instrumental instruction in bluegrass, old-time, Celtic and world musics, along with visual arts, dance, writing, yoga, Indigenous arts and culture, and more. The schedule also includes free evening concerts, a keynote lecture, art gallery talks and additional events.Maryland lawmakers pass 'Stop Super Speeders' bill, awaits Gov. Moore’s signatureTraditions Weeks dates are June 22-26 (virtual) and July 6-10 (in-person). Highlighted instructors this year include Alice Gerrard, Tim O’Brien, Guy Davis, Professor Louie, Tatiana Hargreaves and Reed Stutz, along with 97 additional instructors offering more than 150 weeklong workshops. Organizers describe it as “like the fun, no-exam version of college.”",
